---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import katex from "katex";

const k = (tex: string) => katex.renderToString(tex, { throwOnError: true });
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.endsWith("/")
  ? import.meta.env.BASE_URL
  : `${import.meta.env.BASE_URL}/`;
---

<Base
  title="The Square Achievement Game on n × n Boards"
  description="Two players claim cells of an n by n grid; the first to own four corners of a square wins. The first player wins exactly when n is at least 5, by a rule with no lookup table from 6 by 6 up, machine-checked in Lean. Play against it."
>
  <header class="masthead">
    <h1>The Square Achievement Game on n × n Boards</h1>
    <p class="byline">Sean Reid · September 2026</p>
  </header>

  <p class="lead">
    Two players take turns claiming cells of an
    <span set:html={k("n \\times n")} /> grid. The first to hold all four corners
    of an axis-aligned square wins; a full board with no square is a draw. Erickson
    asked who wins. The answer: the first player wins exactly when
    <span set:html={k("n \\ge 5")} />, and from 6 × 6 up a rule of one paragraph
    wins by the seventeenth ply. The whole argument, every board size at once,
    is checked in Lean 4.
  </p>

  <div class="theorem">
    <span class="label">Theorem.</span> The first player has a winning strategy in
    the square achievement game on the
    <span set:html={k("n \\times n")} /> board if and only if
    <span set:html={k("n \\ge 5")} />. For <span set:html={k("n \\le 4")} /> the game
    is a draw under optimal play. For <span set:html={k("n \\ge 6")} /> the first
    player following the plus rule wins by the seventeenth ply.
  </div>

  <p>
    The plus rule: open at (2,2), answer the first reply with a fixed second
    move, take a completion or a block whenever one exists, and otherwise follow
    the shortest line of at most three single forcing moves ending in the arms
    of a
    <em>plus</em>, a held stone with the four cells at a common distance along
    the axes. Once the centre and one arm are held, each further arm makes three
    corners of a square, so the opponent must block on a diagonal cell, and the
    last arm threatens two squares at once. The rule reads only its own stones,
    the opponent's stones inside a 17 × 17 region, and one derived cell, so the
    same rule serves every board; it plays the same 79 lines on every board from
    6 × 6 to 17 × 17. On 5 × 5 no forcing strategy exists and a table with quiet
    forks plays instead.
  </p>

  <figure class="game">
    <sag-board n="6"></sag-board>
    <figcaption>
      Play against the rule. The bot moves first, explains each move, and draws
      the line it is following; click a cell, or use the arrow keys and Enter.
      Change n to explore: on 5 × 5 the table plays, and below 5 × 5 neither
      side can force a win.
    </figcaption>
  </figure>

  <nav class="routes" aria-label="where to go next">
    <a class="route" href={`${base}downloads/paper.pdf`}>
      <span class="route-title">Read the paper</span>
      <span class="route-body">
        The outcome theorem, the rule in full, why plus arms force, the
        verification runs, the simulation argument that carries one checked
        board to all larger ones, and the formal theorem that the rule's own
        play wins. PDF, built from this repository.
      </span>
    </a>
    <a class="route" href={`${base}proof/`}>
      <span class="route-title">See how the proof works</span>
      <span class="route-body">
        An illustrated guide: pluses and tempo moves, the latent corner, the
        draws and the 5 × 5 board, what a computer search has to be trusted
        with, and how one run on the 23 × 23 board covers every larger board.
      </span>
    </a>
    <a
      class="route"
      href={`${base}downloads/square-achievement-formalization.tar.gz`}
    >
      <span class="route-title">Check it yourself</span>
      <span class="route-body">
        The Lean 4 development. The rule is the definition <code>plusMove</code
        >, 250 lines with no data; a successful <code>lake build</code> is the proof,
        and the README shows how to print the theorems and their axioms.
      </span>
    </a>
  </nav>

  <h2>What you have to trust</h2>
  <p>
    The headline statements in the development read as follows, with
    <code>P1CanWin</code> the first player forcing a win, the two draw predicates
    each player securing at least a draw, and <code>WinsWith σ n h</code> the tree
    in which the first player plays the move of the strategy <code>σ</code> at every
    node after the history <code>h</code>, against every legal reply, ending in
    a first-player square on every branch.
  </p>
  <pre
    class="lean"><code>theorem outcome (n : ℕ) :
    (P1CanWin n ∅ ∅ ↔ 5 ≤ n) ∧
      ((P1CanDraw n ∅ ∅ ∧ P2CanDraw n ∅ ∅) ↔ n ≤ 4)

theorem plus_wins_with {"{"}n : ℕ{"}"} (h : 6 ≤ n) :
    WinsWith plusMove n []</code></pre>
  <p>
    The definitions behind them, cells, squares, the winning predicate as a
    strategy tree, and the 250 lines of <code>plusMove</code>, are the only part
    a skeptic has to read. Beyond them the trust is Lean's kernel, mathlib, and
    <code>native_decide</code>, which lets the Lean compiler evaluate the
    exhaustive checks instead of the kernel. The 5 × 5 table was found by a
    Python search, but nothing about that search enters the proof: a wrong table
    fails the check. The bot on this page is a TypeScript copy of the rule,
    compared with the Lean definition on every reply pair of the 6 × 6 board and
    on the anchored positions of the proof.
  </p>

  <h2>Open questions</h2>
  <p>
    The rule wins by ply 17: how quickly can the first player force a win on the
    <span set:html={k("n \\times n")} /> board, and can one rule of comparable length
    be fastest for every <span set:html={k("n")} />? The rule's second move has
    seven exceptions; is there an opening that needs none? Are the constants of
    the proof, the 6 × 6 plan region and the 17 × 17 base board, least? And 5 ×
    5, where no purely forcing strategy exists, still has no short rule.
  </p>
</Base>
